      not say so.
  
    ''',
  })
# ---
# name: test_refusal[candidate_set_on_some_shards_only]
  dict({
    'exit_code': 1,
    'stderr': '',
    'stdout': '''
      Refusing to merge: Shards [1] record no candidate scanner set while the others do. These results
      were produced by different versions of ASH, so they are not known to have partitioned the same
      scanner set -- and the shards without the record are the ones that could be hiding a gap. Re-run the
      whole matrix on one version.
  
    ''',
  })
# ---
# name: test_refusal[candidate_sets_differ]
  dict({
    'exit_code': 1,
    'stderr': '',
    'stdout': '''
      Refusing to merge: The shards partitioned different scanner sets: shard 0: bandit, semgrep; shard 1:
      bandit, grype, semgrep. Each partition is internally valid, so nothing else here can see this: one
      executor was missing a plugin module, or --python-only or a config override was applied to some jobs
      and not others. Merging would report a scan that never covered the scanners the smaller set omitted.
  
    ''',
  })
# ---
